messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 949 and 786? Please reason step by step.', "<think>.\nTo find the product of 949 and 786, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Here's how we do it step by step:.\n\n1. **Multiply the ones place of the second number (6) by each digit in the first number (949), starting from the rightmost digit:**.\n   - \\(6 \\times 9 = 54\\).\n   - \\(6 \\times 4 = 24\\).\n   - \\(6 \\times 9 = 54\\).\n\n   So, the result of multiplying the ones place of the second number by each digit in the first number is:.\n   \\[.\n   54, 24, 54.\n   \\].\n\n2. **Multiply the tens place of the second number (8) by each digit in the first number (949), starting from the rightmost digit:**.\n   - \\(8 \\times 9 = 72\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 4 = 32\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 9 = 72\\).\n\n   So, the result of multiplying the tens place of the second number by each digit in the first number is:.\n   \\[.\n   72, 32, 72.\n   \\].\n\n3. **Multiply the hundreds place of the second number (7) by each digit in the first number (949), starting from the rightmost digit:**.\n   - \\(7 \\times 9 = 63\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 4 = 28\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 9 = 63\\).\n\n   So, the result of multiplying the hundreds place of the second number by each digit in the first number is:.\n   \\[.\n   63, 28, 63.\n   \\].\n\n4. **Multiply the thousands place of the second number (0) by each digit in the first number (949), starting from the rightmost digit:**.\n   - \\(0 \\times 9 = 0\\).\n   - \\(0 \\times 4 = 0\\).\n   - \\(0 \\times 9 = 0\\).\n\n   So, the result of multiplying the thousands place of the second number by each digit in the first number is:.\n   \\[.\n   0, 0, 0.\n   \\].\n\n5. **Add all the results together, aligning them by their place values:**.\n   \\[.\n   \\begin{array}{cccc}.\n     & 63,28,63 \\\\.\n   + & 72,32,72 \\\\.\n   + & 54,24,54 \\\\.\n   \\hline.\n     & 72,85,45 \\\\.\n   \\end{array}.\n   \\].\n\n6. **Combine the results to get the final product:**.\n   \\[. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
