umber (2) by the hundreds digit of the second number (6). This gives us \\(2 \\times 6 = 12\\). Add the carried over 2 to get \\(12 + 2 = 14\\). Write down the 4 in the hundreds place of the result and carry over the 1 to the ten-thousands place.\n\n9. **Multiply the hundreds place**: Finally, multiply the hundreds digit of the first number (4) by the hundreds digit of the second number (6). This gives us \\(4 \\times 6 = 24\\). Add the carried over 1 to get \\(24 + 1 = 25\\). Write down the 5 in the ten-thousands place of the result and carry over the 2 to the hundred-thousands place. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
